  "query_preview": "..\nThrough Saturday...\n\n500 mb troughing moving along the international border will\nsuppress upper level ridging decreasing heights over the region\ntoday. This appears to be offset by 700 mb downsloping flow \nhowever keeping temperatures in the 90s F. Isolated showers and \nthunderstorms are expected today with steep low level lapse rates\nand increasing deep layer shear. A few severe storms capable of \nproducing hail up to 1 inch and wind gusts up to 60 mph are \nexpected. \n\nMonday will bring a couple cold fronts with a weaker one in the \nmorning being enough to decrease temperatures to the high 80s to \nlow 90s F. A stronger cold front in the afternoon and evening will\nincrease wind gusts into the 20s mph. Monsoonal moisture will be \npushed to the south from the 500 mb trough bringing dry \nconditions. 500 mb troughing is expected to begin to move east \nTuesday allowing monsoonal moisture to return to areas near the \nWyoming border bringing 30% chances for showers and thunderstorms.\nTemperatures are expected to be in the mid 80s to low 90s F. \n\nWednesday through the end of the week is expected to bring the\nreturn of upper level ridging and monsoonal moisture. Temperatures\nin the 90s F and daily chances for showers and thunderstorms are\nexpected. Extremely high PWAT values could bring flash flooding \nconcerns back to the region with the Weather Prediction Center\nissuing a risk level 1 of 4 for excessive rainfall for the\nBeartooth Mountains next week. Torgerson",
